Appendix C — Data Provenance and Source Ledger

C.1 Hyperscale capacity

Synergy Research data, as reported in multiple independent outlets, places the United States at 54–55% of worldwide hyperscale operational capacity. The Q4 2024 figure is 54%, with Europe and China each at approximately one-third of the remainder. The later 2025 figure is 55%, up from 52% three years earlier. The correct claim is single-country dominance, not a two-nation split. The earlier formulation "two nations account for half" is not supported and is replaced by the US-alone formulation.

C.2 The sub-Saharan African gap

Access Partnership's Global South AI Diffusion Playbook estimates that sub-Saharan Africa will face a 98% gap between projected AI inference demand and available data-centre capacity by 2030, with a projected requirement of 4.2 GW against a track of 0.1 GW. The figure is a projection for 2030, not a current measured gap, and the framework states it as such. A separate reporting of the same analysis gives 8.7 GW demand and 0.1 GW supply; the discrepancy between 4.2 GW and 8.7 GW across secondary reports indicates that the underlying scenario parameters are not fully transparent in the public summaries, and the framework therefore reports the gap as a range and attributes it to the source rather than treating it as a point estimate.

C.3 The binary HPC claim

The claim that none of the world's 100 high-performance computing clusters capable of training large language models are hosted in developing countries appears in the Access Partnership summary. The framework does not adopt this as a binary claim. China's classification as developing is contestable in this context; India operates a public GPU compute pool; Brazil and others have substantial HPC capacity. The defensible claim is graded: frontier-scale training capacity is concentrated in a small number of high-income countries and firms, while mid-scale training and fine-tuning capacity is more distributed but still uneven. The binary formulation is removed because it invites falsification on a classification dispute that does not affect the substantive distributional argument.

C.4 Starlink collision-avoidance figures

SpaceX's December 2025 report to the FCC states approximately 300,000 collision-avoidance manoeuvres in 2025, approximately 820 per day. A July 2026 FCC report covering a six-month window states 207,152 propulsive manoeuvres, with a probability-of-collision threshold of $3 \times 10^{-7}$, supplemented by miss-distance criteria. The operator's own published concept of operations reports over 1,000 manoeuvres per day. The figure is dynamic and the framework cites a date-stamped source rather than treating it as a constant. The $3 \times 10^{-7}$ threshold is more than 300 times smaller than the 1-in-10,000 industry benchmark cited in the filing, which is a deliberate conservative safety policy but also a demonstration that a statistical threshold at constellation scale generates physical behaviour at a rate no human review process can match.

C.5 Kessler onset calibration

The economics-of-orbit-use literature calibrates Kessler syndrome onset anywhere between 2040 and 2184, with the precise date highly sensitive to autocatalytic debris-growth parameters. The framework reports the probability as a distribution over parameters, not a point estimate, because the uncertainty is structural rather than statistical.

C.6 Orbital data centre feasibility

The US Government Accountability Office's April 2026 Science and Tech Spotlight states that large space-based data centres have power and cooling needs requiring solar arrays larger than any launched and assembled in space as of April 2026, that cooling solutions at this scale are unproven, that heat does not dissipate efficiently in the near-vacuum of space, and that deployment of some data centre satellites is planned by the mid-2030s. The FCC has received three applications for large satellite constellations operating as data centres since January 2026, with other projects in China, the EU, and Japan. These are applications and plans, not deployed infrastructure. The framework therefore treats large-scale orbital data centres as a prospective design problem and tests its propositions against present orbital AI.

C.7 Meaningful human control

The tracking and tracing conditions derive from the Santoni de Sio and van den Hoven formulation, published in Frontiers in Robotics and AI 5, Article 15 (2018). The tracking condition is a counterfactual reasons-responsiveness condition formalised through subjunctive conditionals. The literature explicitly notes that system-level metrics for meaningful human control are lacking and that operationalisation is an open problem. The protocols in Appendix B are a specification proposal; their reliability is not established and must be tested.

C.8 Incomplete-information viability precedent

The quantifier asymmetry in the observation-feasible condition has precedent in the differential games literature. Cardaliaguet and Souquière's "A differential game with a blind player" (SIAM Journal on Control and Optimization 50(4), 2010/2012) considers a zero-sum differential game in which one player is informed of the initial state while the other knows only a prior distribution and receives no further information. The "blind player" formalises exactly the structure the observation-feasible condition requires: one party observes, the other must act without further observation. The framework cites this precedent rather than claiming the quantifier ordering as novel.

C.9 EU Data Act

Article 29 of the EU Data Act prohibits switching fees, including data egress fees, for customers in the EU from 12 January 2027. Cost-based charges are permitted only until that date. Early contract termination fees and penalties remain permissible after the prohibition. The framework uses this as a natural experiment for the disaggregation of exit cost: the Act removes $\mathrm{EC}^{\mathrm{econ}}$ but does not address $\mathrm{EC}^{\mathrm{temp}}$ or $\mathrm{EC}^{\mathrm{cap}}$.

